<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00172</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cbbaf39a-7f4e-4c89-8ebf-1ec40366f4a4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
        <para id="para-00001"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_1_7_3_PLA_244"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
       
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section class="practice" id="sect-00003"><title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00002"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_1_7_3_PLA_245"/></para>
          </problem>
          </exercise>
         
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
        <para id="para-00049"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_1_7_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>    </note>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Equations Without Solutions</title>
        <para id="para-00050">What happens when we solve <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>?</para>
        <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Subtract <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> to get the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> terms together on one side.</para>
        <para id="para-00052"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>z</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>z</m:mi>
                <m:mi/>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>z</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>z</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Simplify and the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> terms are gone!.</para>
        <para id="para-00054"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mi/>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00055">Since 0 does not equal <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, this equation led to a false
          statement. There are no solutions to this equation.</para>
        <para id="para-00056"><emphasis>Example:</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00057">Solve <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>7</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>11</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00058"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as
          much as possible. Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like
          terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00102"> <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00103"> <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00059"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one
          side of the equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00104">Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
          <para id="para-00105"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00106"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00060"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
        <para id="para-00107"> The equation <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is NOT a true statement. The
          equation 5m+3(9+3m)=2(7m−11) has no solutions.</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00006">
        <title>Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
        <para id="para-00061">What if an equation results in a true statement without variables?</para>
        <para id="para-00062">The equations <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> are all true statements because
          one side is equal to the other. This means that any value of the variable in the original
          equation would be a solution. When this happens in the final step of an equation, there
          are infinitely many solutions.</para>
        <para id="para-00063"><emphasis>Example:</emphasis> Solve <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00065"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as
          much as possible. Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like
          terms.</para>
        <para id="para-00066"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00067"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one
          side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
          <para id="para-00108"> <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00109"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00068"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00069"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check the solution. We say the solution
          to the equation is all of the real numbers.An equation that is true for any value of the
          variable is called an <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">identity</term>.</para>
        <para id="para-00070">The solution of an identity is all real numbers.</para>
      </section>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Equations with No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions </title>
          <exercise id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00071">Solve <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>and
        determine the number of solutions.</para>
            <para id="para-00072">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
        button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00073">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00074">Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
            <para id="para-00075"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00076"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00077"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Distribute</para>
            <para id="para-00078"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00079"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on
        one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
            <para id="para-00080"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi/>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mspace linebreak="newline"/>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi/>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00081"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check the solution</para>
            <para id="para-00082">This is a true statement so there are infinitely many solutions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

          <exercise id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00083">Solve <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> and
        determine the number of solutions.</para>
            <para id="para-00084">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
        button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00085">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00086">Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
            <para id="para-00087"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00088"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00089"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Distribute</para>
            <para id="para-00090"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00091"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on
        one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
            <para id="para-00092"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi/>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                    <m:mspace linebreak="newline"/>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi/>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00093"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check the solution</para>
            <para id="para-00094">The equation <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is NOT a true
        statement. The equation <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
        has no solutions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>